  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nForecast for a a round or two of heavy rain this afternoon \nthrough Tuesday morning remains on track. A strong low pressure \nsystem is evident on WV imagery over the northern Rockies early \nthis morning. This system will progress east into the northern \nPlains today with strengthening southerly flow ahead of the low \ndrawing seasonally high moisture into the region. Expect rain to \ninitially push into central WI during the early afternoon, \nhowever, the exact timing for the onset of precipitation remains \nsomewhat uncertain as forecast soundings do show dry air in the \nlow-levels that will need to be overcome. This initial round will \nlikely be light to moderate widespread rain that spreads across \nthe area by afternoon. A second round of rain is then expected \nbeginning this evenings over central WI as a strong LLJ pushes \nnoises into the region. This second round is expected to bring a \nmore focused area of moderate to heavy rain to central and east- \ncentral WI late this evening and overnight. Between these two \nrounds of rain HREF LPMM 24hr rainfall totals show a corridor of \n2-3\" totals south of a line from Wausau to Sturgeon Bay with 0.5 \nto 1\" north of that line. The exact placement of this higher end \ncorridor may still shift north or south slightly. A few embedded\nthunderstorms are possible with this system, but weak instability\nkeeps the severe threat low. Rain will come to an end from west \nto east Tuesday morning high pressure them bringing dry conditions\nfor the remains of Tuesday and much of Wednesday.\n\nFor the remainder of the week a baroclinic zone is expected set up \nover or just south of the forecast area. This will keep a 40-70% \nchance for showers and occasional thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon \nthrough next weekend, however, it will likely not rain that entire \nperiod. Temperatures will hover near to slightly below normal next \nweek depending on where the boundary sets up each day.",
